Owned source adaptations
-----------------------
The character, Minecraft, writing, and Bilibili tools were adapted from the
project author's local tools. data/provenance.json records source-relative
names and SHA-256 hashes of the reviewed input snapshots. It contains no
absolute installation paths or account credentials. These tools are maintained
as part of this package; the running source deployment is not modified.

The OneBot v11 client is an independent implementation of the public protocol:
https://github.com/botuniverse/onebot-11
It does not import another DSH QQ plugin's implementation.

Runtime dependencies
--------------------
This project has third-party npm dependencies. Their source is not copied into
this package's own lib/tools directories. npm installs the packages separately,
with their original license files. Direct runtime dependencies at this version:

  minecraft-data 3.117.0         MIT
  minecraft-protocol 1.68.0      MIT
  ws 8.22.0                       MIT
  mineflayer 4.39.0               MIT
  mineflayer-pathfinder 2.4.5     MIT
  mineflayer-collectblock 1.6.0   MIT
  qrcode 1.5.4                   MIT
  undici 7.30.1                  MIT

Transitive dependencies retain their upstream notices and licenses. Inspect
package-lock.json and the installed dependency license files for the exact tree.
The host provides React to the DSH client; the standalone development preview
uses the separately installed React/React DOM packages (MIT).

Not included
------------
Minecraft server binaries, game assets/world saves, NapCat, QQ, Java, Python,
FFmpeg, account cookies, platform signing code, and platform internal signing
constants are not distributed by dsh-companion. The Heybox adapter invokes a
user-provided external CLI; no Heybox signing implementation is included.

The setup wizard may download Minecraft server components from Mojang, portable
Eclipse Temurin Java from Adoptium or the version-required Mojang Java runtime,
and the NapCat Windows portable archive from the
official NapNeko GitHub release, only after explicit user confirmation. These
files remain separate user-managed runtime data and retain upstream terms.
The user accepts the Minecraft EULA and completes account login themselves.
NapCat execution requires a packaged version/hash anchor and explicit confirmation.
Only the reviewed portable Node entry point is run; installers and elevation
helpers are never executed. An owned process-local bridge directs the native
QQ data accessor to the character's dedicated profile without native setters.
Paper and SkinsRestorer artifacts are downloaded only when the user selects Paper for the pinned 1.21.8 build, after client-visible acceptance was recorded.


DSH, Minecraft, QQ, NapCat, Bilibili, and Heybox are names of their respective
projects or services. This package does not imply their endorsement.

0.6.0 additions
---------------
Survival decisions, background intents, snapshots, named places, acceptance
checks, and daily ledgers are original implementations based on the supplied
functional requirements. No private persona, diary, account, or machine path
was copied. The optional dsh-proactive integration uses registered tool names
and the schedule-file protocol only; its implementation is neither imported
nor distributed. It is maintained and installed independently.


Privacy
-------
The plugin collects and uploads no data. Persona, diary, memory, media and credentials remain in the user data directory; credentials are never returned by an API. Outbound requests are limited to a hard-coded allowlist of official download hosts, each requiring explicit confirmation. See docs/privacy.md.
